o... I'll keep value \"O\" and maybe an extra attribute environmental_class_note. Simpler: value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". It's a string value, fine.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range (+0.1 °C / +50 °C). The class T50 means water temp 0.1 to 50 °C. I'll record water_temperature_class: \"T50\" and water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. Value given \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → min 0.1 max 50.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure (MAP): 1.6 MPa (16 bar). Record maximum_admissible_pressure: value 1.6, unit MPa. Could also add note. Maybe add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16? Better to keep as value 1.6 MPa; the parenthetical 16 bar is alternate unit. I could include a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_alt: 16 bar. Hmm. I'll just note in value? Keep strict: value 1.6, unit_symbol \"MPa\". Maybe add extra entry \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" only. Or include both as separate characteristics. I'll include both: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa) and a note attribute? I think one characteristic with value 1.6 MPa is fine, but losing \"16 bar\" isn't great. I'll add a separate characteristic `maximum_admissible_pressure_alternate` — that's noisy. Actually many extractors do: {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"} and mention in description. I'll add both entries since it's structured data. Hmm, let me not overcomplicate; I'll include both as two characteristics: maximum_admissible_pressure (1.6 MPa) — and put \"(16 bar)\" as part of... no. I'll just do MPa.\n\nActually, I can do: {\"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", \"note\"?} — schema says {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. No note field. I'll add a second characteristic \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16? That seems hacky but preserves data. I'll skip; MPa is the primary.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" → value \"All positions\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0\" — this is size-dependent → config_level! Axis: bore size. Values: condition \"reduced bore / full bore < DN250\": \"U0 and D0\", condition \"full bore DN250 and larger\": \"U3 and D0\". Actually read carefully: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". So config axis: bore configuration. Two conditions. I'll put in config_level: attribute \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", axis \"meter size / bore configuration\", values: [{condition: \"default (except full bore DN250 and larger)\", value: \"U0 and D0\"}, {condition: \"full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0\"}]. Values are strings so structured form value can be string.\n\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → value string, boolean-ish. I'll record reverse_flow: true? The value is a statement. Record as string \"intended to measure reverse flow\" or boolean true. I'll use value true? Safer: string. I'll do value: true? Hmm. \"Reverse flow: The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → supported_reverse_flow: true. I'll record `reverse_flow: {value: true}`. That's reasonable.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: this is size-dependent → config_level. \n  Reduced bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp40 (0.40 bar); larger than DN50: Δp63 (0.63 bar).\n  (Virtual) Full bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp10 (0.10 bar); DN65–DN200: Δp16 (0.16 bar); DN250 and larger: Δp10 (0.10 bar).\n  \n  config_level entry: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"bore configuration / meter size\", values with conditions:\n  - {condition: \"Reduced bore, DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"} with maybe pressure 0.40 bar... The value includes both class and bar. I could structure: value \"Δp 40 